A||N||8||87ECO5||Rust Repair||||5||
Z||000000||87ECO5||02-13-2001||02:52 PM||||Ran a search but didn't find much in the way of information.  I guess asking about rust on a forum that deals with a plastic-bodied sports car is sort of an oxymoron.<P>Also, this isn't dealing with a Fiero but I figured that, since some of us have to deal with some sort of rust on our frames, the TD&Q section was a good place for this.<P>My wife and I purchased a 1992 Pontiac Sunbird convertible last month.  Overall, the body is in great condition.  However, there are a few spots of rust in the door jams and along the bottom of the doors.  Ironically, the spots are appearing where Ziebart drilled holes to pump their rust prevention fluid into the body.<P>My question is - what is the best way to repair these spots.  I got some good information so far.  Wanted to see what others on here think.<P>I'd like to get this fixed before it becomes visible on the outside.||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroJoe||02-13-2001||09:27 PM||||Zeibart SUCKS!  Never ever get a car done by them.  It is a good marketing ploy, "seal your car up", but it actually hurts more.  My dad and I have repaired more rust than what should have been on two cars so far that have been treated with zeibart.  The problem is, they seal the car in places they shouldnt.  They seal the drain holes where water pathways are supposed to empty, so the water that does get inside a panel, stays in there.<P>As for fixing the rust, you can go about it a number of ways.  First, you'll have to remove  the paint and clean up all the rust that is there.  You are smart to start now, becuase from my expierence, if you see a penny's worth of rust on the outside, there is a nickle's worth once you get in.  Anyway, once you have removed all the paint in the surrounding area, and cleaned up all the rust, you might have to do some Bondo'ing to replace the metal that you removed that was rusted.  Bondo'ing is pretty easy, just dont put too much on or you'll have a really fun time sanding.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  After you have the area rebuilt, and at the same shape as the rest of the panel, you can now prime, sand, and then get a Dupli Color can of the same color car you have and finish it off!<P>Any other questions, drop me an email, and send some pics of the trouble area if you can.<P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR>AIM: Fiero87311, Snowboarder0311<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||02-13-2001||09:33 PM||||Hey, when I ask a non Fiero question, I get bashed..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||02-13-2001||09:51 PM||||Rust is the hardest thing to get rid of.  A lot of sanding, phosphorc acid clean metal prep, the bondo to fill, lots of priming.  More sanding.  I found it satifying work in my younger days, now I have plastic which doesn't rust.<P>I have to agree about Ziebart.  My first new car was a Chevy Vega and I had that coated by Ziebart.  It took me a while to figure out why I kept hearing sloshing liquid when I drove the car.  All the quarter panel drain holes had been plugged with the goop. Plus the spare tire location in the floor kept filling with water. Rust city after 4 years along with a replacement engine after 3 years.  Not one of GM's better designs.<p>[This message has been edited by Old Lar (edited 02-13-2001).]||||reg||2
Z||000004||rogergarrison||02-14-2001||01:26 AM||||I fix a lot of rust spots on dealers trade ins. Almost all of them I use a small sandblaster to clean the spot completely clean. The small ones are very cheap, less than $20. If the pits left are slight, can be filled with primer and sanded, if deeper you will have to use body filler. If theres a hole, seal it up inside with aluminum tape, then filler on outside. if you cant get to the backside of hole, put a swipe of filler on and stick a patch of masking tape over it till it hardens to hold it in place.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Haze_Performance||02-14-2001||06:26 AM||||I used POR-15 rust preventive paint on an old project car. I swear by it!! Its the best s**t Ive ever used. The rust never came back & the finish was hard as nails.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.por15.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.por15.com/</A> <P><BR>As soon as the V8 goes into the '87 SE, Im going to coat EVERYTHING with the stuff..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <BR><P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.ws6fiero.com" TARGET=_blank><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ttttttt.gif"> </A>       <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/WS6_Fiero/photo.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>PFF Members Photo Page</A><BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1431213" TARGET=_blank><BR>Photopoint Gallery</A>||||reg||12
Z||000006||SteveJ||02-14-2001||09:29 AM||||Um, rust. yea. Here in the northeast they salt the roads all winter. All that sheet metal under the battery box rots. So does the the other side. The Bottom of the trunk behind the engine rusts from the outside inward. The door pillars pop rust through from the backside. <BR>'round here you learn the use of fiberglass fabric.<BR>I'm thinking of buying my next car from Arizona or South Carolina.||||reg||1
Z||000007||TheFiero64||02-14-2001||04:18 PM||||Por-15 or Corroless Rust Stabilizer sold by <A HREF="http://www.eastwoodcompany.com" TARGET=_blank>www.eastwoodcompany.com</A>  Someday I want to strip my Fiero to the frame and cover everything with Corroless.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||3
Z||000008||Wannabe||02-14-2001||04:57 PM||||The oil base rust inhibiting products, "Rust Check" or "Krown" are excellent. They recommend a treatment every year, but even two or three treatments during a car's lifetime will dramatically reduce or even prevent rust and corrosion of plated bolts and electrical wires and contacts. A car that's treated with this stuff every year will look brand new after ten years of salty winters. The stuff is messy, but a wash with engine degreaser will clean most of it off and reveal perfectly preserved metal underneath. Highly recommended. It also does wonders where rust has already started.||||reg||1
